**Note**: Please be extremely cautious with these assertions as they can introduce a significant overhead into the test execution time as they halt the execution in case of failure, which can help to catch issues faster but increase the execution time.--------------------It seems like the assertion statements in the C# test code are directly embedded within comments, i.e., "//". I will use a simple regex pattern match in c# to locate these statements and create the equivalent `Debug.Assert()` calls.

The following script can be used to do this task:

```cs
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;
using System.IO;

namespace Program
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            string source = File.ReadAllText("source_file.cs");
            string pattern = @"(// @@@.*[^(\r?\n)])";

            var matches = Regex.Matches(source, pattern);

            foreach (Match match in matches)
            {
                string str = match.Groups[1].Value.Trim();
                Console.WriteLine(str.Replace("//", ""));
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Change "source_file.cs" to the path of your actual test file. The script will read the file, match the strings that start with "// @@", and replace "//" (which signifies a comment in C#) with an empty string. The resulting strings will represent the `Debug.Assert` calls. Please ensure your pattern is correct and that your test file does not contain empty lines or other unrelated assert statements, as they all will be included in the output. 

