Given these requirements, let's firstly say that we are adding x to the collection. The contract for some ArrayList implementation probably includes the insertion operation (which would cause an increase in Count):

```cs
[AssertionMethod]
private static void AddContract(ArrayList arrList, int x) {
    int oldCount = arrList.Count;
    arrList.Add(x);
    int newCount = arrList.Count;
    
    Debug.Assert(newCount == oldCount + 1); // Contract
}
```

In the Remove method:

```cs
[AssertionMethod]
private static void RemoveContract(ArrayList arrList, int x) {
    int oldCount = arrList.Count;
    arrList.Remove(x);
    int newCount = arrList.Count;
    
    Debug.Assert(newCount == oldCount - 1); // Contract
}
```

For Insert and Set methods:

```cs
[AssertionMethod]
private static void InsertContract(ArrayList arrList, int x, int index) {
    arrList.Insert(index, x);
    
    Debug.Assert(arrList.Contains(x)); // Contract
}

[AssertionMethod]
private static void SetContract(ArrayList arrList, int x, int index) {
    arrList[index] = x;
    
    Debug.Assert(arrList.Contains(x)); // Contract
}
```

In Get method:

```cs
[AssertionMethod]
private static void GetContract(ArrayList arrList, int index) {
    int x = (int) arrList[index];

    Debug.Assert(arrList.Contains(x)); // Contract
}
```

For these simple cases, a general contract could look like this: 

```cs
// Assume count will change after each operation
int oldCount = arrList.Count;
// Do the operation
arrList.MethodToTest(...);
int newCount = arrList.Count;

// If this is an add/remove operation, count is expected to increase/decrease by 1
Debug.Assert(newCount == oldCount + 1);
```

Please consider this for the initial proof of concept and do modify according to your implementation. 

The above code is a suggested way to approach it, but note that testing contracts like these can become incredibly complex and labor intensive for complex collection types with potentially many different methods and overloads, and may require you to carefully analyze your implementation contract to know exactly when the counts of your collection should change.--------------------